window.docContents[185].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Proper-smooth-transport":{"contents":"Let  be smooth and proper. Precomposition defines a continuous\nlinear map between the canonical LF test-function spaces,Properness ensures that the inverse image of the compact support of\n is compact.\n\nContinuity is the content of the definition, since the underlying map is simply\ncomposition with . Properness is what keeps  compactly\nsupported. The\nlocalized pullback\ndrops this hypothesis by inserting a cutoff.\n\nFor every test function ,\n\nOn each fixed compact-support stage, the iterated chain rule bounds every\nderivative seminorm of  by finitely many derivative seminorms\nof . The derivatives of  are uniformly bounded on the compact\ninverse image of the support. Mathlib's seminorm criterion therefore gives a\ncontinuous linear map on each compact stage, and the LF universal property\nTestFunction.limitCLM assembles these maps into the stated continuous\noperator.\n\nTransposing the pullback gives a pushforward of distributions, the analogue of\nthe image measure. The transpose of a continuous linear map needs no structure\non the value space beyond continuity of the module operations, so vector values\ncost nothing.\n\nThe transpose of proper test-function pullback is a continuous pushforward\nof distributions,The construction works for distributions valued in any real topological\nmodule  satisfying Mathlib's standard continuity assumptions.\n\nThe action formula is the distributional change of variables. For a regular\ndistribution with density  it is the pushforward of the measure \nunder , and no Jacobian factor appears because the pairing never chooses\ncoordinates on the target.\n\nFor every distribution  and test function ,\n\nApply Mathlib's compact-convergence transpose\nContinuousLinearMap.precompCompactConvergenceCLM to the continuous\ntest-function pullback. Evaluation of the transposed operator is exactly the\ndisplayed pairing formula.\n\nFunctoriality allows a pushforward along a composite map to be computed in\nstages, for instance along a linear map followed by a translation. Both\nidentities are consequences of the action formula and extensionality.\n\nProper smooth pushforward is functorial:\n\nEvaluate both sides on an arbitrary test function. Identity reduces to\n, while composition reduces to\n. Extensionality of\ncontinuous linear functionals gives the two distribution identities.\n\nSupport theory for distributions in this book is organized around carriers\nrather than around the intrinsic support. A carrier is any set outside of which\nthe distribution vanishes, and a compact carrier is the hypothesis that the\nconvolution construction later takes as input.\n\nA distribution  has a compact carrier if there is a compact set \nsuch that  vanishes on every test function supported in .\n\nThe intrinsic support is the intersection of all closed carriers, and a\ncompact carrier can be strictly larger than the support.\n\nIf  vanishes outside a closed set , thenConsequently, a compact carrier makes the intrinsic distributional support\ncompact.\n\nThe distributional support lies in every closed carrier. For a compact carrier,\nthe intrinsic support is therefore a closed subset of a compact set and is\ncompact.\n\nThe first application of the carrier notion is to differential operators.\nDirectional derivatives, hence every finite constant-coefficient operator\n, keep the support of a test function inside any region containing it,\nand after transposition this becomes preservation of vanishing regions.\n\nFor ordinary distributions,  preserves every vanishing region. HenceIf  has a compact carrier, then  has the same compact carrier.\n\nMathlib proves that every iterated directional derivative preserves a\nvanishing region. Scalar multiples and finite sums preserve the same property,\nso the finite operator does as well. The support inclusion follows from the\nvanishing characterization of distributional support, and a compact carrier\ncan be reused unchanged.\n\nThe same argument applies to tempered distributions, with complex coefficients\nand Schwartz probes.\n\nFor tempered distributions and complex coefficients,  preserves every\nvanishing region and cannot enlarge support:\n\nMathlib's tempered iterated-derivative support theorem applies term by term.\nThe vanishing identity is stable under complex scalar multiplication and\nfinite sums, and the intrinsic support inclusion follows from the same\nvanishing characterization.\n\nPushforward interacts with carriers through images: a test function supported\naway from  pulls back to one supported away from . Because proper\nmaps are closed, the image of a closed carrier is again a closed carrier, and\nthe support inclusion follows.\n\nSuppose  vanishes outside a closed set  and  is smooth and proper.\nThen  vanishes outside , and\n\nIf a test function  is supported outside , its pullback\n is supported outside ; the pushforward action formula therefore makes its pairing with\n vanish. Proper maps are closed maps, so  is a closed carrier.\nThe distributional support therefore lies inside that carrier.\n\nAddition  is not proper when , so the convolution\nconstruction cannot use proper pushforward directly. For compactly carried\ndistributions a cutoff removes the properness hypothesis.\n\nSmooth proper pushforward preserves compact carriers. In particular, if \nhas a compact carrier, then  has compact distributional support.\n\nChoose a compact carrier  for . The preceding theorem transports the\nvanishing property to , while continuity of  makes  compact.\nThe intrinsic support is closed and lies in this compact carrier, hence is\ncompact.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Distributions and Fourier Analysis\u0009Canonical distributions","header":"3.4.1.8. Proper smooth transport","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis--Canonical-distributions--Proper-smooth-transport"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems":{"contents":"\n\nFor a first-order system, the principal symbol determines characteristic\nspeeds and directions. Symmetry turns that algebraic object into a\nself-adjoint operator, so the finite-dimensional spectral theorem gives\nhyperbolicity and the PDE admits an energy identity.\n\nThe energy argument has three stages: symbol algebra, a local energy-flux\nbalance, and integration in space and time. Its estimates concern a given,\nsufficiently regular solution.\n\nFor a systemthe principal symbol at the covector  is\n.\n\nFreezing the coefficients at a point and inserting a plane-wave ansatz reduces\nthe system to the eigenvalue problem for this symbol, so the classification of\nthe system is phrased entirely through its spectrum.\n\nA system is hyperbolic at  when, for every real covector , the\nsymbol has a real eigenbasis:It is hyperbolic when this holds throughout the PDE domain. Strict\nhyperbolicity additionally requires  for\n whenever .\n\nStrict hyperbolicity is the stronger notion, but its defining condition is\nimposed only for , so the implication to plain hyperbolicity needs a\nseparate remark about the zero covector.\n\nPointwise and domain-wide strict hyperbolicity imply the corresponding\nhyperbolicity predicates:\n\nFor nonzero covectors, discard the pairwise-distinctness condition from the\nstrict hyperbolicity data and retain its real eigenbasis. At the zero\ncovector the symbol is the zero operator, for which the standard basis is an\neigenbasis with eigenvalue zero.\n\nVerifying the eigenbasis condition covector by covector is rarely practical.\nSymmetry of the coefficient operators is a checkable pointwise condition that\nimplies it.\n\nA symmetric system has a self-adjoint coefficient operator  in\nevery spatial direction at every point of its PDE domain.\n\nIf  for every spatial direction, thenEvery symmetric first-order system is therefore hyperbolic.\n\nReal linear combinations of self-adjoint coefficient operators are\nself-adjoint, so every principal symbol is self-adjoint. The\nfinite-dimensional spectral theorem supplies an orthonormal basis of real\neigenvectors, exactly the pointwise hyperbolicity condition.\n\nSymmetry does more than diagonalize the symbol. Pairing the equation with \nturns each term  into half a spatial derivative\nof  plus a correction involving , so\nthe density  obeys a local balance law with an explicit flux\nand an explicit production term.\n\nThis is the pointwise form of the integration-by-parts energy calculation on\nEvans pp. 426-427. At a point where the coefficients are symmetric, the field\nand coefficient maps are differentiable, and the system equation holds, the\nidentity keeps the variable-coefficient correction\n. The domain-wide form\nsupplies pointwise symmetry from a symmetric system.\n\nPair the system equation with . Symmetry rewrites\n as one half of the derivative of\n, minus the derivative-of-coefficient correction.\nThe time term is one half of , giving the local balance.\n\nIntegrating the local balance over a spatial slice removes the flux\ndivergence when the spatial flux has compact support. Differentiating the\nslice energy also requires domination of the time derivative, as specified\nin the next theorem.\n\nUnder explicit dominated-differentiation hypotheses, pointwise\ndifferentiability and the system equation, and compactly supported \nspatial flux slices, the spatial energy is differentiable and\n\nThe dominated-differentiation hypothesis moves the time derivative through\nthe spatial integral. Integrate the local balance over space; each compactly\nsupported flux divergence has zero integral, leaving only forcing and the\nvariable-coefficient production term.\n\nThe exact balance is not yet an estimate, because the production term has no\nsign; it is controlled by a linear expression in the energy.\n\nIf  almost everywhere bounds\n, the integrated production satisfieswhere .\nThe production, the forcing energy, and the solution energy are assumed\nintegrable on the slice.\n\nCauchy–Schwarz and Young's inequality bound\n by one half of the forcing and solution squares.\nOperator norm bounds control each coefficient-derivative term by its norm\ntimes . Summing the pointwise inequalities and integrating gives the\nclaim.\n\nOn a closed time interval, assume continuous total energy, the exact-balance and\nproduction-bound hypotheses at each earlier time, , and\nthe initial energy is at most . Mathlib's Grönwall inequality then\ngives\n\nCombine the exact energy derivative with the production bound to obtain\n. The initial bound\n and continuity of  put this in Mathlib's right-derivative\nGrönwall form, whose explicit solution is the stated bound.\n\nThe estimate points to uniqueness. The operator is linear, so the difference\nof two solutions with the same forcing and initial data solves the problem\nwith zero data, where . The weak-solution construction\nalso proves uniqueness in .\n\nThe energy method controls solutions without exhibiting any. For constant\ncoefficients and no forcing the symbol's eigenvalue problem produces explicit\nsolutions, profiles transported along a linear phase.\n\nFor a homogeneous constant-coefficient system, the vector-profile wavesolves the equation at a differentiability point exactly when\n there.\n\nThe chain rule gives  and\n. Substitution into the constant-coefficient operator\nproduces , whose vanishing is exactly the displayed\neigenvalue relation.\n\nTaking  with a fixed polarization vector separates the profile\nfrom the symbol condition.\n\nFor  the equation holds exactly\nwhen  at the point or the polarization satisfies\n. The latter relation gives a solution for every\ndifferentiable scalar profile.\n\nFor , differentiation gives\n. The vector-profile condition factors as\n, so it holds precisely when either factor\nvanishes.\n\nImposing the eigenvector condition at every profile parameter, rather than at\none point, upgrades the pointwise substitution to a solution of the\ninitial-value problem.\n\nIf the vector profile is differentiable and its eigenvalue condition holds\nfor every profile parameter, the plane wave solves the initial-value problem and has trace\n at .\n\nApply the pointwise substitution theorem at every profile parameter to obtain\nthe homogeneous PDE. Evaluating the phase at  removes the\n term and gives the claimed initial trace.\n\nFor a polarized profile the eigenvector condition  is a\nsingle algebraic identity independent of the profile.\n\nIf  and  is differentiable, thensolves the homogeneous constant-coefficient initial-value problem and has the\nexact trace .\n\nBy linearity, the eigenvalue hypothesis gives the eigenvalue condition for the\nvector profile at every phase, whatever the value of\n, so the vector-profile initial-value theorem gives the PDE and the trace.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws","header":"7.5.1. Symmetric First-Order Systems","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Symmetric-First-Order-Systems"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature":{"contents":"A quadrature certificate bounds the difference between a proposed value and\nthe integral. Exactness on polynomials and bounds on derivatives control\nthe analytic error; interval evaluation controls the error in computing the\nfinite weighted sum. These two errors add.\n\nA quadrature certificate stores a proposed value of a Bochner interval\nintegral, an explicit error budget, and a proof of the corresponding norm\nestimate. The certificate is indexed by the ordinary Lean integrand and\nendpoints, so no symbolic expression type appears in theorem statements.Its trusted field is\n\nA complete certificate: Simpson on .\n\nTake  on . One Simpson panel uses the values at\n, , and , so its exact-real value is\n\n\n\nBecause Simpson's rule is exact for every polynomial of degree at most three,\nits quadrature certificate has:\n\n* the stored value is ;* the stored error is ;* soundness proves .\n\nThe third field is the mathematical content. The first two fields are data\nthat later planners can inspect and combine. Since the integrand is a\ncontinuous polynomial, it is also interval integrable; that fact is separate\nbecause QuadratureCert itself is stated for Mathlib's totalized interval\nintegral.\n\nFor a nonpolynomial integrand, the shape of the object is unchanged. A\nSimpson or Gauss–Legendre theorem supplies a positive analytic error, and a\nverified numerical backend may replace the exact-real weighted sum with a\ncomputed value by adding a separately proved roundoff error. The final\nsound field always states one inequality about the original integral.\n\nThe first polynomial beyond Simpson's exactness range shows what the error\nconstant measures. For  on ,\n\n\n\nHere , so the sharp Simpson bound is\n, exactly the observed error. The fourth derivative\nmeasures the first behavior that the degree-three exactness does not\nreproduce.\n\nIf both functions are interval integrable, uniform approximation on\n transports through interval integration with error\n. Consequently a quadrature certificate for a\nfinite or symbolic model becomes a certificate for the original integrand,\nwith model and quadrature errors added explicitly.\n\nIntegrate the pointwise difference and apply the norm bound for an interval\nintegral. Uniform approximation bounds the integrand norm by\n, while the integral of that constant over the oriented\ninterval contributes . A final triangle inequality adds this model\nerror to the existing quadrature error.\n\nIf the scalar integrand is interval integrable and one backend interval contains every value on\n, its midpoint defines a constant integral model and its radius gives\nthe uniform pointwise error. The resulting certificate has value\n and error , with no endpoint-order assumption and no\nbackend-specific integration theorem. Exact rational ranges, directed\nfloating-point ranges, Taylor enclosures, and adaptive local ranges therefore\nshare the same analytic bridge.\n\nWrite the enclosure as center  and radius . Interval membership gives\n uniformly, so integration stability compares  with the\nconstant function . The constant integral is , and its\ntransported error is .\n\nHow the error budget is assembled.\n\nA certificate constructor assembles the error budget in five steps:\n\n1. prove exactness on a polynomial model or prove a Peano-kernel remainder;2. obtain any required derivative bound as an ordinary theorem;3. sum local analytic errors when the interval is subdivided;4. enclose the finite weighted sum if it is evaluated numerically;5. add the proved roundoff radius to the analytic budget.\n\nThe quadrature rule chooses nodes and weights. The derivative theorem controls\ntruncation. The numerical backend controls finite evaluation. Keeping those\nproofs separate makes it possible to replace one backend or one subdivision\npolicy without changing the statement about the integral.\n\nQuadrature rules implemented.\n\nThe certificate constructors use four normalized rules, each stored as an\nexact list of (weight, node) pairs on . Each error formula assumes\nthe stated derivative bound and the corresponding  or \nregularity on an ordered interval .\n\n* Trapezoidal rule: two nodes at  with weights .\n  Exact for polynomials of degree at most 1. Error on :\n   where  bounds .* Midpoint rule: one node at  with weight . Exact for polynomials\n  of degree at most 1. Error on : , half the\n  trapezoid constant.* Simpson's rule: three nodes at  with weights\n  . Exact for polynomials of degree at most 3. The sharp\n  Peano-kernel error on :  where  bounds\n  .* Two-point Gauss–Legendre: two nodes at\n   with weights\n  . With only two nodes it is exact for polynomials of degree\n  at most 3, like Simpson's three-node rule. The sharp Peano-kernel error:\n  , which is  of Simpson's error constant.\n\nThe Gauss–Legendre rule has irrational nodes (), which\nare represented exactly in the real-valued QuadratureRule. Numerical\nevaluation encloses the exact weighted terms by proved intervals. A\nRationalQuadratureRule, by contrast, has rational nodes.\n\nThe Peano kernel approach.\n\nThe sharp error constants (1/2880 for Simpson, 1/4320 for Gauss–Legendre) come\nfrom the Peano kernel method. For a rule  that is exact on polynomials of\ndegree at most  and an integrand , the integration\nerror equals\n\n\n\nwhere  is the Peano kernel of order : the functional\n applied to the truncated power\n. If  has constant sign, the integral is bounded\nby .\n\nExample: composite Simpson on .\n\nFirst plan the exact-real composite value to analytic tolerance\n. A computed replacement must also fit its evaluation error\nwithin the remaining budget before claiming this same total tolerance.\n\n1. Derivative bound. On , . Set .2. Error constant. .3. Panel planning. Solve :\n   .\n   The planner rounds up to : exactly,\n   .4. Certificate construction. Build 18 Simpson panels of width .\n   Summing their sharp errors gives\n   .5. Value computation. The panel-major schedule contains 54 weighted samples,\n   with  distinct nodes because adjacent endpoints coincide.\n   A numerical backend must provide enclosures for those samples and their\n   weighted sum; the analytic panel count alone supplies no evaluation precision.6. Final certificate. The QuadratureCert stores:* value: the midpoint of the weighted-sum enclosure,* error: the analytic Simpson error plus the weighted-sum roundoff radius,* sound: the proof that .\n\nThis final error is at most  only if the certified evaluation radius\nis at most . Otherwise, refine the evaluation or\nreplan the analytic mesh with a smaller reserved budget.\n\nAdaptive quadrature and the tree structure.\n\nComposite rules use a fixed number of equal panels, which may be inefficient\nfor integrands with localized difficulty (near-singularities, rapid\noscillation). The adaptive framework addresses this with a binary\nQuadratureTree whose leaves are local certificates and whose branches split\nan interval at a point. Because soundness depends only on the leaf\ncertificates and their composition, an adaptive algorithm may choose the\nsubdivision by any heuristic, including Richardson extrapolation, derivative\nestimates, or a priori knowledge of integrand behavior.\n\nA normalized quadrature rule is an ordered list of exact-real weights and\nnodes in . The order remains explicit for verified finite-precision\nevaluation. Generic definitions transport the rule affinely to any oriented\ninterval, while moment identities characterize exactness independently of a\nparticular numerical backend.\n\nThe rational algebraic fragment of the shared expression language has a\nfail-closed exact evaluator. Every successful result is proved equal to the\nexisting real expression semantics. A quadrature rule with rational weights\nand nodes can therefore sample a symbolic integrand exactly, preserve the\nordered rule schedule, and cast term-for-term to the existing transported\nQuadratureRule. Transcendental nodes, malformed literals, and sampled zero\ndenominators are rejected. Optional\nfinite-precision backends consume the exact table through the same interval\nand weighted-sum interfaces.At the expression boundary,For a successfully compiled rule, casting every rational term preserves the\nanalytic schedule and value:\n\nProve the rational evaluator correct by structural induction on the algebraic\nexpression, rejecting precisely the division cases with zero denominator.\nApply that equality at each rational quadrature node. Casting commutes with\nthe finite products and sum, so the compiled ordered table has the same real\nweighted value as the analytic rule.\n\nIf a normalized rule has the exact moments through degree , it exactly\nintegrates every real polynomial of degree at most . Affine transport\npreserves this conclusion on arbitrary oriented intervals. Both the three-node\nSimpson rule and the two-point Gauss–Legendre rule have the exact moments\nthrough degree three, hence exactly integrate every cubic and generate\nzero-error QuadratureCerts.\n\nExpand a polynomial of degree at most  in the monomial basis. Linearity of\nboth integration and the weighted rule reduces exactness to each monomial,\nwhere it is exactly the assumed moment identity. Affine substitution\ntransports the normalized-interval result to any oriented interval.\n\nIf two scalar integrands differ uniformly by  on\n, their values under a normalized rule differ by at most\n, where  is the sum of the absolute rule weights.\nThis estimate lets a rule that is exact on a nearby model be evaluated on the\noriginal integrand while accounting separately for integral-model error and\nsample-value perturbation.\n\nAt every node, the sample difference is at most . Apply the\ntriangle inequality to the weighted sum, bound each term by\n, and sum to obtain  on the normalized\ninterval. Affine transport contributes the factor .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Integration","header":"10.8.1. Certified Quadrature","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature--Gaussian-quadrature":{"contents":"The degree of exactness of a rule with  nodes can never reach .\nThe nodal polynomial  has degree , so\n has degree ; the rule returns ,\nwhile . Gauss's theorem says that degree  is\nreached, for one choice of nodes only.\n\nWhy must the nodes be the roots of ? Suppose a rule with nodes\n is exact to degree . For any  of degree below  the\nproduct  has degree below , and it vanishes at every node,\nso\n\n\n\nThus  is monic of degree  and orthogonal to all lower degrees,\nand uniqueness forces . Conversely, take the nodes to be the\nroots of  and the weights interpolatory. Any  of degree below\n divides as  with . The rule sees only\n, since  vanishes at the nodes, and it integrates  exactly;\nthe integral sees only  as well, since . So\n.\n\nLet  be continuous on  and strictly positive on ,\n, and let . There are  distinct nodes in  whose\ninterpolatory weights  are all strictly positive and\nfor whichAs a NodalRule, the rule is positive, has its nodes in , and is\nexact to degree .\n\nTake the nodes to be the roots of . The interpolatory rule on \ndistinct nodes reproduces every polynomial of degree below . For\n, divide by the monic nodal polynomial: the quotient term is\nannihilated by the rule, because the nodal polynomial vanishes at the nodes,\nand by the integral, by orthogonality; the remainder has degree below\n. For the weights, apply exactness to , which has degree\n: the rule returns , and the integral\n is positive.\n\nThe nodes determine the weights, and the weights are positive. This\npositivity will give convergence for continuous integrands.\n\nA Gaussian rule with  nodes for the weight  on  is a\nstructure GaussianRule a b w hw n, where hw records that  is\ncontinuous on . It holds nodes , weights\n, and four facts: the nodes are pairwise distinct, they lie\nin the open interval , the weights are strictly positive, andIts value on a function  is . For  the rule is\nempty and the exactness field says nothing, so the theorems about Gaussian\nrules assume .\n\nDistinct nodes give the Lagrange and Hermite interpolants behind the\ncharacterization and the remainder formula. Interior nodes let a bound\nthat holds on  be applied at every node, and positive weights turn such\na bound into the stability and convergence estimates.\n\nFor , a weight that is strictly positive on , and ,\ngaussianRule is the Gaussian rule of the existence theorem, with nodes at the\nroots of  and interpolatory weights. Every Gaussian rule with  is a\npositive NodalRule exact to degree . Exactness on the constant \nmakes its weights add up to , so each weight is at most\n.\n\nFor every Gaussian rule with  nodes for a continuous weight that is\nstrictly positive on , the nodal polynomial  is the\nmonic orthogonal polynomial  of , and the weight at  is the\ninterpolatory weight , the Christoffel number. A Gaussian\nrule is therefore unique up to the order of its nodes.\n\nThe nodal polynomial times any  of degree below  has degree below\n and vanishes at every node, so its integral is its rule value, zero.\nUniqueness of the monic orthogonal polynomial identifies the nodal\npolynomial. For the weights, apply exactness to , which has degree\n and takes the value  at  and  at the other nodes.\n\n\n\nThe figure shows both halves of the argument for  and the Legendre\nweight. On the left,  has degree four; the rule returns zero,\nand orthogonality makes the integral zero too. On the right,  has\ndegree six; the rule still returns zero, but the integral is\n. That positive number reappears as the constant in the\nerror formula.\n\nThe error of a Gauss rule is found by comparing  with its Hermite\ninterpolant , the polynomial of degree below  that matches  and\n at the  nodes. The rule is exact on  and cannot tell \nfrom , so the error is . The pointwise remainder is\nthe familiar one for interpolation, with every node counted twice:\n\n\n\nBecause , a mean-value argument for the integral pulls the\nderivative out at a single point.\n\nLet  be continuous on  and strictly positive on ,\n, let  be a Gaussian rule with  nodes, and let\n on . There is  withwhere  is the nodal polynomial, equal to  by the\ncharacterization. Here  is the iterated derivative within , so smoothness\nis needed only on the closed interval. If  on ,\nthe error is at most .\n\nFix  away from the nodes and choose  so that\n vanishes at . Then  has double zeros at the \nnodes and a simple zero at ,  zeros counted with multiplicity. A\ngeneralized Rolle theorem, proved for a chain of functions each the\nderivative of the previous one on the open interval, gives a zero \nof . Since  and , this is\nthe pointwise remainder. Multiply by  and integrate. The continuous\nfunction  takes values between its minimum and maximum on\n, so the integral of  lies between those extremes times\n, and the intermediate value theorem produces .\nFinally  by the characterization.\n\nFor  and the Legendre weight the constant is\n. Rescaled to an interval of length ,\nwhere  scales by , the formula gives error\n, the same constant as the sharp two-point\nGauss–Legendre certificate, which is proved by a Peano kernel. For  the\nconstant on\n is .\n\nThe first four Gauss–Legendre rules have closed forms in radicals. On\n the one-point rule is the midpoint rule with weight , the\ntwo-point rule has nodes  and weights , the three-point rule\nhas nodes  and weights , and the\nfour-point rule has nodes  with weights\n, the larger weight at the inner pair. The library stores\nthem in the normalized convention on , checks the moments degree by\ndegree, and then identifies each with the orthogonal-polynomial\nconstruction.\n\nThe normalized three- and four-point Gauss–Legendre rules, written in\nradicals, are exact through degrees five and seven. Transported to\n, the midpoint rule, the two-point rule, and these two rules are\nGaussian rules for the unit weight, and the nodal polynomial of the\n-point rule is the monic Legendre polynomial of degree , defined as\nthe monic orthogonal polynomial of that weight, for . For\n and any , every Gaussian rule for the unit weight on \nhas the transported two-point nodes and weights and the same value as the\ntwo-point QuadratureRule.\n\nExactness through degree  is a finite list of moment identities, each\nchecked by normal-form arithmetic using  or the\nsquares of the four-point radicals. Positivity of the weights and the ordering\nof the nodes are inequalities between radicals. The characterization theorem\nthen identifies each nodal polynomial with the monic Legendre polynomial,\nand for  it pins down the nodes, whose weights are then fixed by\nexactness.\n\nBeyond four points the nodes are no longer convenient radicals, and the\nlibrary certifies them instead. A sparse rational polynomial  with\n for , a condition checked in rational\narithmetic from the moments of , is the monic Legendre polynomial of\ndegree  as soon as it is monic of degree . Its roots are isolated by\ncertified brackets (root brackets): a rational\ninterval with a sign change at the ends and a derivative enclosure of fixed\nsign, so exactly one root lies inside. Ordered disjoint brackets inside\n then define a Gaussian rule at any order , with no stored table\nof nodes.\n\nLet  be a sparse rational polynomial and let  valid root brackets for\n be ordered, each strictly to the left of the next. The bracketed roots\nare strictly increasing and lie in their brackets. If  is monic of degree\n over , its nodal polynomial on these roots is  itself.\nIf moreover the brackets lie inside  and the rational moments\n vanish for , the roots are the nodes of a\nGaussian rule for the unit weight on , with the interpolatory\nweights.\n\nEach valid bracket contains a unique root, and the ordering of the brackets\norders the roots. A monic polynomial of degree  with  distinct roots\nis the product of the corresponding linear factors. The vanishing moments,\nextended linearly to every  of degree below , make the nodal\npolynomial orthogonal to lower degrees; the Gaussian exactness and weight\npositivity arguments then apply without change.\n\nThe weights of a certified rule are enclosed from the same brackets. At a\nsimple root  of  the Lagrange basis polynomial is\n, so the Christoffel number is\n\n\n\nSynthetic division writes the integral as a polynomial in  with\nrational coefficients, so the weight is a rational function of the single\nroot , and interval evaluation over the bracket encloses it.\n\nIf the nodal polynomial of a family of nodes is  and  is a root,\nthe interpolatory weight at  is , where\n is the rational polynomial integratedQuotient p with\n. For a valid bracket the rational\ninterval weightInterval p bracket contains this quotient at the bracketed\nroot, and each Christoffel number of the certified rule lies in the interval\ncomputed from its own bracket.\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomial at a node is the nodal polynomial divided by\n and by the nodal weight, which equals  for a simple root.\nThe division by  is carried out monomial by monomial through\n, and integrating the result term\nby term gives . The bracket's direction fixes the sign of\n on it, so dividing the numerator enclosure by the derivative enclosure\nis sound.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Certified Integration\u0009Certified Quadrature","header":"10.8.1.3. Gaussian quadrature","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Certified-Integration--Certified-Quadrature--Gaussian-quadrature"}});